1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:21,925
Marcellus made our wedding rings in a room that smelled of polished
wood, hot metal, and the excellent coffee he refused to let Tank touch
with sugar. "Nigga," Tank said, looking into the cup, "why it taste
like a serious decision?" "Because you drink syrup," Marcellus
replied. Rosa laughed and took the cup from her fiancé.

2
00:00:22,175 --> 00:00:43,086
"Give him something sweet before he proposes to the biscuit tray." We
sat around the jeweler's worktable while morning light came through
the windows. Outside, Vegas prepared for the wedding with the patience
of a city that had already decided patience was somebody else's
problem. Florists called. Drivers called.

3
00:00:43,336 --> 00:01:09,500
A man from the sound crew called to ask whether the Sky High choir
required separate support for the bass section because one member
could accidentally levitate equipment when enthusiastic. Rosa said
yes, then demanded to know why nobody had asked that question before
ordering the stage. My wife sat beside me, turning our original
wedding ring around her finger. "We keeping those," I said.

4
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:25,476
"Of course." "Nigga, I survived too much for a trade-in policy." She
smiled. "The new ones go with them." Marcellus set two narrow bands on
the velvet between us. Mine was white gold with a thin blue line
through the center.

5
00:01:25,726 --> 00:01:43,095
Shawna's matched it, finer at the edges, bright enough to catch the
light when she moved without trying to compete with the ring she
already wore. Inside mine, tiny letters read: I CAME BACK. Inside
hers: I WAS HERE. I looked at her. She held my gaze.

6
00:01:43,345 --> 00:02:05,713
Nigga, I had crossed continents looking for ways to say more, and my
wife had put the whole fucking thing inside four words. "You choose
that?" I asked. She nodded. "You like it?" I tried to make a joke.
Nothing came. Shawna smiled, softer now. "There he is." Tank leaned
forward. "What mine say?" Rosa covered her ring with one hand.

7
00:02:05,963 --> 00:02:26,929
"You find out at the wedding." "Nigga, I need time to prepare
emotionally." "You had months." "I used them on flowers." Marcellus
laughed and brought out their rings. Tank's was broad, dark gold,
built for a hand that had never encountered a delicate situation it
could not accidentally turn into furniture.

8
00:02:27,179 --> 00:02:49,981
Rosa's held a small blue stone surrounded by warm gold, a ring that
looked good beside both a microphone and a fist. She stared at it.
Tank stared at her staring. Nigga, that was his favorite part of being
rich. Not the price. The second before the woman he loved found her
voice again. "Baby," she said quietly.

9
00:02:50,231 --> 00:03:06,635
"Yeah?" "You did good." The nigga looked down at his coffee like he
needed somewhere private to put his face. I put a hand on his
shoulder. "Don't fall apart before the fitting. We need your
measurements stable." He laughed and shoved my hand away.

10
00:03:06,885 --> 00:03:23,117
After they left to argue with the tailor, Shawna took Dante's watch
off her wrist and laid it beside the rings. Marcellus picked it up
carefully. "The clasp?" "A little loose," she said. "After the
bridge." He examined it beneath the lamp.

11
00:03:23,367 --> 00:03:40,058
I watched his hands move over the watch he had known in a different
part of the story, before I stood inside it wearing a dead man's cross
and wanting a living woman's whole heart. The old jealousy did not
rise. That surprised me for half a breath.

12
00:03:40,308 --> 00:03:59,969
Then I realized it had been quiet for a long time and I had finally
stopped listening for it. Shawna looked at me. "What?" "Nothing."
"Nigga, that's not your nothing face." I touched the cross beneath my
shirt. "I was thinking about the first night. That watch on your
wrist." She smiled faintly.

13
00:04:00,219 --> 00:04:19,526
"You thought it meant I was leaving." "I thought a lot of stupid shit
with excellent confidence." "Past tense?" "Nigga, let me grow for a
minute." She laughed. Marcellus took the watch into the adjoining
workroom, leaving us alone at the table. Shawna rested her head
against my shoulder.

14
00:04:19,776 --> 00:04:44,882
"I'm wearing it tomorrow." "I know." "And your rings." "I know that
too." "And a dress you're going to embarrass yourself about." I turned
toward her. "Nigga, why you hide the important information at the
end?" She kissed me. The watch ticked softly from the other room while
Marcellus worked. It was an ordinary sound now. A little machine
keeping time. No accusation. No contest.

15
00:04:45,132 --> 00:05:07,380
Just something a woman I loved had carried through days I could not
change and into a morning where she had already chosen where to sit. I
put my arm around her. "You think he would've liked me?" I asked. She
considered it seriously. Then she smiled. "He would have thought you
were unbearable." "Nigga, fair." "And he would have laughed." "At me?"
"With you.

16
00:05:07,630 --> 00:05:28,493
Eventually. After you stopped introducing yourself to every mirror."
"That's an important professional relationship." She laughed against
my chest. I held her there until Marcellus returned. He handed back
the watch, repaired and shining. Then he opened a small box of his
own. Blue Mercy lay inside.

17
00:05:28,743 --> 00:05:51,388
The stone caught the light with that deep blue fire I had seen men
lose their minds trying to own. It had crossed prisons, marriages,
lies, dead hands, rich rooms. Now it rested in the palm of the man
whose love had given it meaning before any of those niggas tried to
put a price on it. "You wearing it?" I asked. He nodded.

18
00:05:51,638 --> 00:06:11,023
"At the wedding." "Good." "I have kept it in the pouch long enough."
He slid the ring onto his finger. It fit. The old man's hand trembled
slightly, then steadied. Shawna reached across and touched it.
"Beautiful." "She was," he said. We did not correct him. He took
Celeste's letter from a drawer.

19
00:06:11,273 --> 00:06:34,353
The paper had been folded and unfolded so many times its creases
seemed part of the handwriting. He looked at her name at the bottom,
smiled to himself, and put it back beside a photograph. Then he closed
the drawer. "Now," he said, "show me what you intend to wear with my
work." Nigga, the man had returned to his profession. I respected the
shift.

20
00:06:34,603 --> 00:06:58,813
The afternoon fitting took place at the mansion because the tailor had
looked at the guest list and decided his shop was not structurally
prepared. Tank stood on a low platform in a blue suit while three
people circled him with pins. He held his arms out like a dangerous
angel waiting for somebody to finish making wings. "Too tight?" the
tailor asked. Tank flexed.

21
00:06:59,063 --> 00:07:21,834
A seam made a small sound. The tailor closed his eyes. "Please do not
answer physically." I laughed from the next platform. "Nigga, he
asked." My own suit was midnight blue, dark enough to look black until
the light found it. The jacket fit around the sling Vega still
insisted I wear when I was not pretending the doctor could not see me.

22
00:07:22,084 --> 00:07:40,556
A narrow opening inside kept the badge where I could reach it, because
Knox said she refused to attend a wedding where her best officer had
to undress before becoming a problem. The tailor adjusted my collar.
Then he stepped back. Then farther back. "What?" I asked.

23
00:07:40,806 --> 00:08:06,734
"I need perspective." "Nigga, it's a suit." "On you, Captain, it has
become an event." Tank pointed at him. "That nigga understands the
assignment." Rosa came in to check the colors. She saw Tank. Stopped.
Then put both hands over her mouth. Tank looked down at himself,
alarmed. "Baby?" She shook her head. The nigga stepped off the
platform so fast the tailor nearly fainted.

24
00:08:06,984 --> 00:08:32,984
"What's wrong?" "Nothing," she said through her fingers. "You look—"
She could not finish. Tank went still. Then he smiled, slow and
astonished. I looked away before the moment became something I had
stolen by watching too closely. On the terrace, Knox and Seraphine
shared a bottle of wine while discussing the logistics of putting a
floating choir over a city full of fireworks enthusiasts.

25
00:08:33,234 --> 00:08:57,292
"They will remain at a sensible altitude," Seraphine said. Knox looked
at me through the open doors. "My understanding of sensible has
suffered recently." "The captain does encourage revision." "That's one
word." They laughed together. Two women who had spent years commanding
rooms found it pleasant to sit in one where nobody needed rescuing for
at least twenty minutes.

26
00:08:57,542 --> 00:09:21,680
Magnolia and Bernard argued over table placements. Cassius and Inez
arrived with both fathers, who immediately began correcting each
other's memories of old races. Mercedes and Porsche brought a guest
list covered in lipstick marks and changes. Liora called from the
First Dance to say the airship was ready. Sienna's musicians rehearsed
in the garden.

27
00:09:21,930 --> 00:09:45,462
June and Adrian arrived late, tired, and smiling like two niggas
hiding a mechanical surprise. Shawna remained upstairs. I tried to go
find her. Rosa blocked the stairs. "No." "This my house." "And that
dress is none of your business until tomorrow." "Nigga, I'm the
groom." "That's why you under restriction." Tank came up beside me.

28
00:09:45,712 --> 00:10:07,932
"We could go through the balcony." Rosa looked at him. He stepped
backward. "I was identifying a security concern." I laughed so hard
the tailor shouted from the other room that my shoulders were moving
again. That night, after the guests left and the house quieted, Shawna
came downstairs wearing one of my shirts. No wedding dress.

29
00:10:08,182 --> 00:10:27,931
No jewels except the rings she already wore and the watch ticking on
her wrist. She sat beside me on the sofa and put her feet in my lap.
"You ready?" I looked at her. Nigga, I had been ready in a motel with
a bullet in my side. Ready in a hospital while pretending pain did not
frighten me.

30
00:10:28,182 --> 00:10:46,692
Ready on a floating school, on a racecourse, on a broken bridge with
my hands refusing to work. But that night I was ready in a quieter
way. No gun in my hand. No door to break. Just the woman, the house,
and the next morning. "Yeah," I said. She smiled.

31
00:10:46,942 --> 00:10:57,091
"Me too." We stayed on the sofa until the city lights went soft beyond
the windows, and for once I let tomorrow arrive without trying to beat
it there.
